---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import civFrame1Img from '../../assets/images/civ/civ_frame1.png';
import civCcImg from '../../assets/images/civ/civ_cc.png';
import civCcCloudImg from '../../assets/images/civ/civ_cc_cloud.png';
import civBlenderImg from '../../assets/images/civ/civ_blender.png';
---
<PageLayout title="Greece: Shadows of the Past — Alex Sheen">
  <div class="w3-padding-64 w3-content" id="photos">
    <div class="w3-padding-16 white_text" style="margin:0 -16px">

      <div class="w3-row">
        <div class="w3-rest">
          <div class="proj" style="width:100%">
            <div class="projtitle" style="width:100%">
              Greece: Shadows of the Past
              <div class="projtext" style="width:100%">
                <br />
                <div class="vl">
                  Blender, AliceVision Meshroom<br />
                  2023<br />
                </div>
                <br />
                Is it possible to escape the shadow of the past in Greece today?
                <br /><br />
                The reality is that the Greek state has already erased certain aspects of its own past
                to create a homogenous and unifying memory that dominates public perception of Greece, both locally and abroad.
                To escape the undesirable shadows of its past, Greece has attempted to establish a national identity around a continuous
                history from Classical, Byzantine, to Modern Greece, selecting certain memories of the past while ignoring all others.
                Whether it is through the removal of non-Classical structures on the Athenian Acropolis or the forced
                renaming of towns from Slavic to Greek names, these attempts have largely demonstrated success in shaping perceptions by the
                average individual. This work walks through a photogrammetric reconstruction of the Ancient Athenian Agora and the adjacent
                Adrianou street, a site where historical and modern strata meet in tension. By shifting and dissolving these strata, the work
                visualizes the steps the modern Greek State has already taken to curate its historical narratives and exposes the voices and
                cultural vibrancies that will be overwritten by a continuation of such efforts.
                <br /><br />
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=greece_video">
        <Image src={civFrame1Img} style="width:100%;" alt="Greece video thumbnail" />
      </a>

      <div class="w3-row">
        <div class="w3-rest">
          <div class="proj" style="width:100%">
            <br />
            Sanitization of Greek History
            <div class="projtext" style="width:100%">
              <br />
              In its efforts to root its nation's foundations in the Classical period, the Greek state has metaphorically and physically destroyed
              aspects from its past to create a sanitized version that permeates popular culture both locally and internationally. The modern
              presentation of the Athenian Acropolis, for example, has stripped the site of any non-Classical or non-structural elements. Lacking the
              metal adornments, free-standing statues, polychromatic colors, and post-Classical buildings that should be visible, the current Acropolis
              is a barren, white-washed, curated image that has become the embodiment of Greece as a whole, one that attracts millions of
              tourists each year.
              <br /><br />
              In recent years, there has been increased pushback against the destructive tendencies that arise from such historical sanitization.
              In 2019, the Greek courts blocked the demolition of two Art Deco era buildings on Dionysiou Areopagitou Street in Athens.
              <br /><br />
              New city plans have been designed with western grid systems with Neoclassical architecture that expunge any Ottoman influence.
              Most impactful, perhaps, has been Konstantine Paparrigopoulos' <i>History of the Hellenic Nation from the Ancient Times Until the Modern</i>,
              which traces an unbroken history from Classical, Byzantine, and Modern Greece.
              <br /><br />
              Alcock, Susan. <i>Archaeologies of the Greek Past: Landscape, Monuments, and Memories</i>. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2002.
              <br /><br />
              Camp, John. "The Athenian Agora: A Short Guide." Accessed May 27, 2022.<br />
              http://www.agathe.gr/icons/pdfs/agorapicbk-16.pdf
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="w3-row">
        <div class="w3-half">
          <div class="proj" style="width:100%">
            <br />
            Documentation | Process
            <div class="projtext" style="width:100%">
              281 images of the Ancient Athenian Agora were processed with photogrammetric software AliceVision Meshroom to
              produce a 3D mesh reconstruction. The 3D mesh was then modified manually in Blender3D to replace missing pieces
              and to imagine how the Agora might extend below the modern street.
              <br /><br />
              The 3D mesh reconstruction was then processed with CloudCompare to produce a uniform-density point cloud.
              <br /><br />
              The resulting point cloud was then animated and rendered in Blender3D.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="w3-half">
          <div class="proj_rel" style="width:100%">
            <br />
            <div class="projtext" style="width:100%">
              <Image src={civCcImg} style="width:100%;" alt="CloudCompare" />
            </div>
            <br /><br />
            <div class="projtext" style="width:100%">
              <Image src={civCcCloudImg} style="width:100%;" alt="Point cloud" />
            </div>
            <br /><br />
            <div class="projtext" style="width:100%">
              <Image src={civBlenderImg} style="width:100%;" alt="Blender" />
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="w3-row">
        <div class="w3-rest">
          <div class="proj" style="width:100%">
            Documentation | Photogrammetry
            <div class="projtext" style="width:100%">
              Computation Time - 19 hours<br /><br />
              Meshroom ran on UChicago's Peanut Cluster using 281 photos taken from the Ancient Agora in Athens, Greece.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="proj" style="width:100%">
            Documentation | Animation
            <div class="projtext" style="width:100%">
              Render Time - 50 hours (~2:30 per frame for 1200 frames)<br /><br />
              Video fly-through animated and rendered using Blender's Cycles. The point cloud was imported using <a href="https://github.com/uhlik/bpy">Point Cloud Visualizer</a> add-on
              with Blender3D Version 2.83.0.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
